## Best Practice for Preventing Scroll While Keeping the Scrollbar Visible in a Modal Overlay (2026)

As of 2026, there are **three modern CSS-first techniques** that work together as the recommended approach. The ecosystem has matured significantly, and pure-CSS solutions have largely replaced JavaScript-based scroll-locking.

---

### 1. ✅ **`overscroll-behavior: contain` on `<dialog>` (The New Gold Standard)**

Introduced effectively in **Chrome 144** (late 2025) and spreading to other browsers, this is the cleanest solution when using the native `<dialog>` element:

```css
dialog {
  overscroll-behavior: contain;
}

dialog::backdrop {
  overflow: hidden;
  overscroll-behavior: contain;
}
```

**Why it's preferred:** It's purely declarative CSS — no JavaScript toggle classes, no saving/restoring scroll position. The browser handles preventing scroll chaining from the dialog back to the page. The scrollbar on the body **remains visible** because you never set `overflow: hidden` on the body itself.

> Source: [Bramus (Chrome DevRel), Nov 2025](https://www.bram.us/2025/11/25/use-overscroll-behavior-contain-to-prevent-a-page-from-scrolling-while-a-dialog-is-open/) and [CSS-Tricks, Dec 2025](https://css-tricks.com/prevent-a-page-from-scrolling-while-a-dialog-is-open/)

---

### 2. ✅ **`scrollbar-gutter: stable` to Prevent Layout Shift**

Even when you do need to toggle `overflow: hidden` on the body (e.g., for non-`<dialog>` modals), use `scrollbar-gutter` to **reserve the scrollbar space** so the layout doesn't jump:

```css
html {
  scrollbar-gutter: stable;
}
```

This ensures the gutter (space where the scrollbar sits) is **always reserved**, even when the scrollbar disappears due to `overflow: hidden`. No more content shifting sideways.

Supported in all modern browsers as of late 2024 (Chrome, Firefox, Safari).

> Source: [MDN Web Docs](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/scrollbar-gutter), [Go Make Things, Sep 2025](https://gomakethings.com/prevent-scrollbar-jump/)

---

### 3. ✅ **`body:has(dialog[open])` for Conditional Scroll Lock (Fallback)**

For broader browser support or non-`<dialog>` modals, the `:has()` pseudo-class lets you conditionally disable scrolling without any JavaScript:

```css
html {
  scrollbar-gutter: stable;  /* Prevent layout shift */
}

body:has(dialog[open]) {
  overflow: hidden;  /* Lock scroll when dialog is open */
}
```

This is the recommended fallback when `overscroll-behavior: contain` isn't fully supported across your target browsers.

> Source: [Frontend Masters Blog (Chris Coyier)](https://frontendmasters.com/blog/scroll-locked-dialogs/)

---

### Complete Recommended Solution (2026)

Combining all three techniques for maximum compatibility:

```css
/* 1. Always reserve scrollbar space to prevent layout shifts */
html {
  scrollbar-gutter: stable;
}

/* 2. Modern: contain scroll within the dialog (Chrome 144+) */
dialog {
  overscroll-behavior: contain;
}

dialog::backdrop {
  overflow: hidden;
  overscroll-behavior: contain;
}

/* 3. Fallback: lock body scroll when dialog is open */
body:has(dialog[open]) {
  overflow: hidden;
}
```

---

### What to Avoid (Legacy Patterns)

| ❌ Old Pattern | Why it's outdated |
|---|---|
| `body { position: fixed; }` | Resets scroll position, causes iOS bugs |
| `padding-right: 15px` on body | Hardcoded scrollbar width — varies across OS/browser |
| JavaScript `wheel` event `preventDefault()` | Brittle, hurts accessibility, unnecessary with CSS solutions |
| `overflow: hidden` on body without `scrollbar-gutter` | Causes layout shift when scrollbar disappears |

---

### Key Takeaway

In 2026, the best practice is a **layered CSS-only approach**: use `overscroll-behavior: contain` on the `<dialog>` element as the primary mechanism, pair it with `scrollbar-gutter: stable` on `html` to prevent layout shifts, and add `body:has(dialog[open]) { overflow: hidden }` as a cross-browser fallback. This eliminates the need for JavaScript scroll-locking libraries entirely.

