## Best Practice for Preventing Scroll While Keeping the Scrollbar Visible in a Modal Overlay (2026)

There are **three tiers** of solutions, from the most modern and recommended to the most broadly compatible:

---

### 🥇 1. Best: `overscroll-behavior: contain` on `<dialog>` (CSS-only, Chrome 144+)

As of **Chrome 144** (late 2025), `overscroll-behavior` now works on **non-scrollable scroll containers**. This is the cleanest, most modern, zero-JavaScript solution. It prevents background scrolling while the dialog is open **and the scrollbar remains visible** (no layout shift).

```css
dialog {
  overscroll-behavior: contain;
}

dialog::backdrop {
  overflow: hidden;          /* Makes the backdrop a (non-scrollable) scroll container */
  overscroll-behavior: contain;
}
```

**How it works:** Setting `overflow: hidden` on `::backdrop` turns it into a non-scrollable scroll container. Then `overscroll-behavior: contain` prevents scroll chaining from reaching the page behind it. The page's scrollbar stays visible because you never touch `overflow` on `<html>` or `<body>`.

**Refinement with `:modal`:** You can scope this only to modal dialogs (not non-modal ones) using the `:modal` pseudo-class:

```css
dialog:modal {
  overscroll-behavior: contain;
}
dialog:modal::backdrop {
  overflow: hidden;
  overscroll-behavior: contain;
}
```

> **Browser support caveat:** As of early 2026, this works in Chromium-based browsers. Firefox ([bug](https://bugzilla.mozilla.org/show_bug.cgi?id=1837436)) and Safari ([bug](https://bugs.webkit.org/show_bug.cgi?id=243452)) have open tracking bugs. Use the next approach as a cross-browser fallback.

---

### 🥈 2. Cross-Browser Fallback: `body:has(dialog[open])` + `scrollbar-gutter: stable`

This works in **all modern browsers** today and is the recommended cross-browser approach:

```css
html {
  scrollbar-gutter: stable;   /* Always reserves space for the scrollbar */
}

body:has(dialog[open]) {
  overflow: hidden;            /* Locks the scroll when any dialog is open */
}
```

**How it works:**
- `scrollbar-gutter: stable` on `<html>` **permanently reserves** the scrollbar's width in the layout, even when `overflow: hidden` is set. This means the scrollbar space remains, preventing the jarring **layout shift** (content jumping sideways) that normally happens when hiding overflow.
- `body:has(dialog[open])` uses the CSS `:has()` selector to detect when any `<dialog>` has the `open` attribute, and locks the body scroll.

This combination **prevents scrolling AND prevents the layout shift** — the scrollbar gutter stays even though the actual scrollbar disappears.

> **Note:** `scrollbar-gutter: stable` reserves space for *classic* (always-visible) scrollbars. On macOS with overlay scrollbars enabled, there is no classic scrollbar, so the gutter has no visual effect — but there's also no layout shift on those systems anyway.

---

### 🥉 3. Legacy Fallback (pre-`:has` / pre-`scrollbar-gutter`): JS + padding compensation

For older browser support, the traditional JavaScript approach still works:

```javascript
// On modal open:
const scrollbarWidth = window.innerWidth - document.documentElement.clientWidth;
document.body.style.overflow = 'hidden';
document.body.style.paddingRight = `${scrollbarWidth}px`;  // compensate for scrollbar removal

// On modal close:
document.body.style.overflow = '';
document.body.style.paddingRight = '';
```

This manually measures the scrollbar width and adds equivalent padding to prevent the layout from shifting. It's what libraries like Bootstrap and Headless UI have done for years.

---

### Summary Table

| Approach | JS needed? | Layout shift? | Browser Support (2026) |
|---|---|---|---|
| `overscroll-behavior: contain` on dialog + backdrop | ❌ No | ❌ None | Chromium 144+ |
| `scrollbar-gutter: stable` + `body:has(dialog[open])` | ❌ No | ❌ None | All modern browsers |
| JS `overflow: hidden` + padding compensation | ✅ Yes | ❌ None (manual) | Universal |

---

### Recommendation

**Use approach #2 (`scrollbar-gutter: stable` + `:has()`) as your baseline** — it's pure CSS, works cross-browser, and fully prevents layout shift. **Layer approach #1 on top as a progressive enhancement** for Chromium users where even the gutter trick isn't needed. Only fall back to #3 if you must support browsers without `:has()` support (effectively IE or very old browsers at this point).

