## Best Practice for Preventing Scroll While Keeping the Scrollbar Visible in a Modal Overlay (2026)

There are **three modern, complementary techniques** that represent the current best practice. Used together, they provide a robust, cross-browser solution:

---

### 1. ✅ **`overscroll-behavior: contain` on `<dialog>` + backdrop** (The New Gold Standard)

Introduced with Chrome 144 (late 2025) and expected to land in all major browsers in 2026, this is now the **cleanest CSS-only approach** for native `<dialog>` modals:

```css
dialog {
  overscroll-behavior: contain;
}

dialog::backdrop {
  overflow: hidden;
  overscroll-behavior: contain;
}
```

**Why it's best:** It prevents the page from scrolling underneath the open dialog *without* toggling `overflow: hidden` on the `<body>`, which means the scrollbar never disappears and there is **zero layout shift**. No JavaScript required.

> **Browser support caveat:** As of early 2026, this enhanced behavior (working on non-scrollable containers) is confirmed in Chromium-based browsers (Chrome 144+, Edge). Firefox and Safari adoption is expected but should be verified. Use this as a progressive enhancement alongside the techniques below.

---

### 2. ✅ **`scrollbar-gutter: stable` on `<html>`** (Prevent Layout Shift)

If you still need to use the `overflow: hidden` approach (e.g., for custom non-`<dialog>` modals), **reserve the scrollbar space** so hiding the overflow doesn't cause a content jump:

```css
html {
  scrollbar-gutter: stable;
}
```

**Browser support:** Chrome 94+, Edge 94+, Firefox 97+, Safari 18.2+. ~93%+ global coverage as of 2026.

This ensures the gutter (space where the scrollbar sits) is **always reserved**, even when `overflow: hidden` removes the scrollbar. The page content doesn't shift horizontally.

---

### 3. ✅ **`body:has(dialog[open]) { overflow: hidden }` with `:has()` selector** (Fallback)

For browsers that don't yet support the `overscroll-behavior` enhancement on non-scrollable containers, combine `:has()` with `overflow: hidden`:

```css
html {
  scrollbar-gutter: stable;  /* prevents layout shift */
}

body:has(dialog[open]) {
  overflow: hidden;  /* locks scroll when dialog is open */
}
```

This is a **pure CSS** solution (no JS needed) that works today across all modern browsers. The `scrollbar-gutter: stable` rule ensures the scrollbar space is preserved even when overflow is hidden.

---

### Complete Recommended Solution (2026)

```css
/* 1. Always reserve scrollbar space to prevent layout shifts */
html {
  scrollbar-gutter: stable;
}

/* 2. Fallback: lock body scroll when a dialog is open */
body:has(dialog[open]) {
  overflow: hidden;
}

/* 3. Progressive enhancement: native scroll containment (Chrome 144+) */
dialog {
  overscroll-behavior: contain;
}

dialog::backdrop {
  overflow: hidden;
  overscroll-behavior: contain;
}
```

### For non-`<dialog>` custom modals (React portals, etc.)

Use a JS toggle with the same CSS principles:

```css
html {
  scrollbar-gutter: stable;
}

body.modal-open {
  overflow: hidden;
}
```

```js
function openModal() {
  document.body.classList.add('modal-open');
}

function closeModal() {
  document.body.classList.remove('modal-open');
}
```

---

### Summary of Key Points

| Technique | Purpose | Browser Support (2026) |
|---|---|---|
| `overscroll-behavior: contain` | Prevents background scroll natively | Chromium 144+; Firefox/Safari TBD |
| `scrollbar-gutter: stable` | Reserves scrollbar space, prevents layout shift | ~93%+ global |
| `body:has(dialog[open]) { overflow: hidden }` | CSS-only scroll lock fallback | All modern browsers |
| `overflow: hidden` + JS class toggle | Works for any modal type | Universal |

**Bottom line:** In 2026, the best practice is to use `scrollbar-gutter: stable` on `<html>` to prevent layout shifts, combined with `overscroll-behavior: contain` on the dialog/backdrop for native scroll locking (progressive enhancement), and `body:has(dialog[open]) { overflow: hidden }` as a reliable cross-browser fallback.

